# Dendra HTTPS reverse proxy (automatic Let's Encrypt certificates).
#
# Prerequisites (operator):
#   1. DNS: two DNS-only A records on Cloudflare (grey cloud, not proxied: Caddy needs the ACME
#      HTTP-01/TLS-ALPN challenge to reach the VPS directly):
#         testnet-api.dendranetwork.com    A  <VPS_IP>   (DNS only)
#         testnet-proof.dendranetwork.com  A  <VPS_IP>   (DNS only)
#      No `chat.` site: the host that carries the only validator serves no chat interface.
#      A site block whose name does not resolve to this host is not harmless: Caddy retries its
#      certificate forever and fills the log with ACME failures.
#   2. Firewall: ports 80 and 443 open (80 = ACME challenge + HTTP->HTTPS redirect, 443 = HTTPS).
#
# Caddy then obtains + auto-renews certificates and reverse-proxies to the internal services over the
# docker network. The upstream services stay HTTP inside the network; TLS is terminated here.

{
	email hello@dendranetwork.com
}

# Shared security headers. Declared as a snippet and imported at site level (outside the `handle_path`
# blocks), so they apply to every response, errors and preflights included, without interfering with the
# per-route CORS `header` blocks.
# No CSP here on purpose: `api` and `proof` serve JSON, which a CSP does not protect. The static website is
# served by its own host, with its own header file, and is not part of this repository.
(security_headers) {
	header {
		# HTTPS required for one year. No `preload`: that entry is irreversible on a short horizon.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		# Never let the browser guess a MIME type.
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		# Do not leak the originating URL to a third party.
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		# Nothing served here is meant to be embedded, and no page of the site uses an iframe.
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		# Drop the server version banner (smaller fingerprint).
		-Server
	}
}

# Chat-completions gateway (/v1/chat/completions). Two entry points on the same host:
#   /demo/*  Public showcase path used by the chat (app. and the site). Caddy injects the Bearer key on the
#            server side, so the browser never sees it and no key can be scraped from the page.
#            Unauthenticated on purpose: a browser request from another site is refused (403), and
#            the rest is bounded by the gateway's free-tier quotas (subsidised from Reserve, testnet
#            tokens). Delete this block to close the public demo.
#   /*       Normal integrator endpoint: bring your own key (Authorization: Bearer ...).
testnet-api.dendranetwork.com {
	import security_headers

	# CORS. A browser refuses a cross-origin response unless the response itself allows it, so every
	# route a web page is meant to call carries these headers, including the preflight: a separate
	# request the browser sends first, which never reaches the upstream.
	# A route without them fails completely, and only in a browser. curl succeeds, which is why a
	# missing header survives every command-line check.
	#
	# Two regimes, on purpose:
	#  - /demo/* is unauthenticated (Caddy injects the project's own key) and its cost is charged to
	#    the project's free-tier quota. It echoes only an allow-listed origin, and it refuses any other
	#    origin with 403 before the gateway (see the block). As a consequence, a `*.pages.dev` preview
	#    deploy cannot reach /demo.
	#  - Everything else is either read-only public data or a bring-your-own-key endpoint. "*" is
	#    correct there: without cookies there is no ambient authority, so a third-party page can only
	#    spend a key it already holds. Restricting it would break legitimate browser integrators and
	#    protect nothing.
	# No Access-Control-Allow-Credentials anywhere: the browser sends a Bearer header it set itself,
	# never a cookie. Adding it would forbid "*" and grant ambient authority that does not exist today.
	#
	# Every matcher below is declared inside its own handle block, never once at site level. Caddy
	# refuses a named matcher defined twice ("matcher is defined more than once: @preflight"), and
	# `/rpc*` and `/rest*` already declare their own. A site-level @preflight makes the whole file
	# unparseable, so Caddy fails to start and api and proof go down together.
	# Validate before deploying: caddy validate --adapter caddyfile --config <file>
	handle_path /demo/* {
		# The origin echo below only decides whether a browser lets the page read the response. It does
		# not stop the browser from sending the request: a form post or a text/plain fetch needs no
		# preflight, and it would open a paid job on the project's key for every visitor of that page.
		# So a request whose Origin header is present and is not an admitted origin is refused here, before
		# the gateway, preflight included, and carries no CORS header. "null" (a sandboxed frame, a local
		# file) is refused too. The test reads the joined value: a request carrying several Origin headers
		# arrives as "a,b", matches no entry, and is refused, even when one of them is admitted.
		# Admitted: the two hosts of the site and app., which serves the chat. explorer. is not listed:
		# it only reads public routes, which answer "*", and never posts here.
		# A request without Origin (curl, a server-side client) is not a browser
		# sent by another site: it passes and stays bounded by the gateway's per-device and daily quotas.
		# The gateway also refuses a body that is not application/json (415), a second bound.
		@foreign_origin {
			header Origin *
			not expression `{http.request.header.Origin} in ['https://dendranetwork.com', 'https://www.dendranetwork.com', 'https://app.dendranetwork.com']`
		}
		respond @foreign_origin 403
		# Same joined-value test: the header directive runs before respond, so a per-value match would
		# put CORS headers on the 403 above.
		@site_origin expression `{http.request.header.Origin} in ['https://dendranetwork.com', 'https://www.dendranetwork.com', 'https://app.dendranetwork.com']`
		@preflight method OPTIONS
		header @site_origin {
			>Access-Control-Allow-Origin "{http.request.header.Origin}"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type, Authorization"
			>Access-Control-Max-Age "86400"
			# Required when the origin is echoed rather than fixed: without it a shared cache can
			# hand one origin's response, headers included, to a different origin.
			+Vary "Origin"
		}
		respond @preflight 204
		reverse_proxy gateway:8651 {
			header_up Authorization "Bearer {$DENDRA_API_KEY}"
		}
	}
	# Chain RPC and REST for browser apps (web wallet, explorer): HTTPS and CORS, so an https:// page can
	# query the chain without mixed content. Read-only public data, exactly what :26657 and :1317 already
	# serve: no key, no secret. `/rpc*` rather than `/rpc/*` because CosmJS POSTs to the bare base.
	# `>` sets or replaces the header instead of adding one. Tendermint and the REST API may already emit
	# their own Access-Control-Allow-Origin, and a second one makes the browser reject the response
	# ("Failed to fetch") even though curl succeeds; the explorer page hit exactly that.
	# Origin is "*" on purpose: these endpoints are read-only and public (no cookie, no auth, no secret).
	# Restricting them protects nothing and breaks legitimate front-ends (preview deploys, www., local
	# development). The paid path (/demo) keeps its strict origin.
	handle_path /rpc* {
		header {
			>Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type"
		}
		@preflight method OPTIONS
		respond @preflight 204
		reverse_proxy chain:26657
	}
	handle_path /rest* {
		header {
			>Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type"
		}
		@preflight method OPTIONS
		respond @preflight 204
		reverse_proxy chain:1317
	}
	# Network capacity registry: nodes POST their hardware probe here, and the /network page GETs the
	# aggregate. `handle`, not `handle_path`: the upstream serves the /capacity path itself.
	handle /capacity* {
		# Read-only public aggregate, like /rpc and /rest: no key, no secret. The /network page and the
		# chat's capacity panel both GET it from the browser.
		@preflight method OPTIONS
		header {
			>Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, OPTIONS"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type"
		}
		respond @preflight 204
		reverse_proxy capacity:8092
	}
	# Final Testnet Season programme. Read-mostly public data; every write is signed by a
	# miner's operator key, so "*" grants nothing a page could abuse. `handle`, not `handle_path`: the
	# service serves the /final-season prefix itself. Its /internal route is not under this prefix and is
	# never proxied.
	handle /final-season/* {
		@preflight method OPTIONS
		header {
			>Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type, X-Dendra-Miner, X-Dendra-Height, X-Dendra-Pubkey, X-Dendra-Sig, X-Dendra-Acct, X-Dendra-Seq"
		}
		respond @preflight 204
		reverse_proxy final-season:8093
	}
	# The network's description files, read-only and with no key: the same bytes publish_network.sh writes and
	# the plain-HTTP server on :8088 serves, here over TLS. Declared before the catch-all, which answers 401.
	handle /network-info.txt {
		root * /srv/published
		file_server
	}
	handle /genesis.json {
		root * /srv/published
		file_server
	}
	handle {
		# Bring-your-own-key endpoint. "*" lets any chat-completions client call it from a browser:
		# the caller's page sends a key it already holds, and no cookie is ever attached.
		@preflight method OPTIONS
		header {
			>Access-Control-Allow-Origin "*"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Methods "GET, POST, OPTIONS"
			>Access-Control-Allow-Headers "Content-Type, Authorization"
			>Access-Control-Max-Age "86400"
		}
		respond @preflight 204
		reverse_proxy gateway:8651
	}
}

# The Proof: read-only verifiability feed (recent jobs, pools, VRF health; no secrets).
testnet-proof.dendranetwork.com {
	import security_headers
	reverse_proxy proof:8090
}
